@startuml
entity Entity01 {
  * identifying_attribute
  --
  * mandatory_attribute
  optional_attribute
}
@enduml
Again, this is the normal class diagram syntax (aside from use of entity instead of class). Anything that you can do in a class diagram can be done here.
The * visibility modifier can be used to identify mandatory attributes.

This page is for Chen's Entity Relationship notation, which is commonly used in teaching. ... Attributes can be keys, meaning that their value is unique among entities of a given type, or they can be derived, meaning that their value is computed based on other attributes. Attributes may also be multi-valued, or have their domain (set of allowed values) defined.
@startchen
entity DIRECTOR {
  Number : INTEGER <<key>>
  Name {
    Fname : STRING
    Lname : STRING
  }
  Born : DATE
  Died : DATE
  Age : INTEGER
}
entity CUSTOMER {
  Number : INTEGER <<key>>
  Bonus : REAL <<derived>>
  Name : STRING <<multi>>
}
@endchen

A weak entity does not have a key attribute that uniquely identifies each instance of that entity. Instead, it is identified by the combination of a partial key on the weak entity itself and the key of another entity, which it is related to via an identifying relationship. A weak entity must have total participation in its identifying relationship.
@startchen
entity PARENT {
  Number <<key>>
  Name
}
entity CHILD <<weak>> {
  Name <<key>>
  Age
}
relationship PARENT_OF <<identifying>> {
}
PARENT_OF -1- PARENT
PARENT_OF =N= CHILD
@endchen

The cardinality of relationships can also be expressed as a range.
@startchen
...
RENTED_TO -(1,N)- CUSTOMER
RENTED_TO -(0,1)- MOVIE
@endchen

namespace net.dummy #DDDDDD {
  .BaseClass <|-- Person
  Meeting o-- Person
  .BaseClass <|- Meeting
}
namespace net.foo {
  net.dummy.Person <|- Person
  .BaseClass <|-- Person
  net.dummy.Meeting o-- Person
}

You can use the interface, annotation and enum keywords too.
@startuml
...
enum TimeUnit {
  DAYS
  HOURS
  MINUTES
}
...
@enduml
